<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahilyabai Holkar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1725">1725</a>–<a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Ahilya Bai Holkar · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holkars">Holkars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahilyabai Holkar was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> queen who ruled the Holkar domains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, later Indore State, from 1767 until her death in 1795. Governing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maheshwar">Maheshwar</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a>, she was known for able administration and justice, and for building and restoring temples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and rest houses across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, including the Kashi Vishwanath temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> in 1780.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahilyabai was born on 31 May 1725 in the village of Chandi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, to Mankoji Shinde, the village <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patil">Patil</a>, and Sushila Shinde. She had five brothers and was first educated at home. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malhar-rao-holkar">Malhar Rao Holkar</a>, a commander under the Peshwa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a> and ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, saw her at a temple service on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> and advised his son Khanderao to marry her. They were wed in 1733, when he was nine or ten and she seven or eight.</p><p>Her mother-in-law, Gautama Bai Holkar, trained her in administration, accounts and politics and in 1759 gave her a khasgi, or land grant. From 1754 Malhar Rao involved her in diplomacy and the kingdom's finances. In a letter of 1765 he ordered her to take heavy artillery to Gwalior and attack the fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gohad">Gohad</a>, and the attack succeeded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Khanderao was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fire in 1754 while inspecting his troops at the siege of the Kumher fort. Ahilyabai announced that she would commit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a> on his pyre, but her father-in-law begged her to live, and she did. Malhar Rao died on 20 May 1766. Her only son, Male Rao Holkar, was invested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> as ruler of Indore on 23 August 1766 and died on 5 April 1767. Malhar Rao's adopted son Tukoji Rao Holkar was then made Subahdar on payment of a large tribute, and Ahilyabai became the de facto ruler.</p><p>Groups from neighbouring states, particularly the Chundawat clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, rebelled during the transfer of power, taking advantage of the devastation that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a>. Ahilyabai led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> armies against them and, despite a lack of resources, won every battle. Her daughter Muktabai committed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a> on the funeral pyre of Yeshwantrao Fanse in 1791. She died on 13 August 1795, aged 70, and Tukoji Rao, her commander, succeeded her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She is credited with repairing and adding to several temples, among them the Kashi Vishwanath Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, and with the Dashashwamedh Ghat at Banaras. Her charities, paid from personal funds estimated at sixteen crore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>, included temples, ghats and almshouses at Dwarka, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>, Haridwar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badrinath">Badrinath</a> and Puri. She also supported women weavers who made Maheshwari saris.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> wrote in 1946 that her reign of thirty years was remembered as a period of perfect order and good government, and John Malcolm, who collected oral memories of her in the 1820s, called her one of the purest rulers that ever existed. John Keay calls her the philosopher queen. Indore's airport and university carry her name, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> issued a stamp in her honour on 25 August 1996.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahilyabai Holkar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ahilyabai Holkar</b> (ruled 1767–95) became known for just administration and for rebuilding temples and ghats across India, from Kashi to Somnath.</blockquote><a href="/state/indore-state">Indore State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jamkhed, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Indore, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.72,75.32&amp;year=1770" aria-label="Jamkhed on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6589 1470 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ahilyabai Holkar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7039 1740h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7039 1740h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.72,75.32&amp;year=1770">What was here in 1770? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
